{
  "suite": "pace_cam",
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "description": "PACE Chip Authentication Mapping. Upstream runs the GM flow under the CAM OIDs and receives-and-ignores the DO8A chip-authentication data (its extraction is a commented-out block, PACEHandler.swift:358-365 vanilla); so CAM completes as plain GM there and there is no upstream oracle for the verify. The port goes beyond parity: it scans the final GA response for the DO8A (tag-scan, not upstream's tvlResp[1] index; P11 §4.4.5 Table 4 lets the conditional DO87/DO88 CARs precede it), decrypts it per P11 §4.4.3.5.3/.4 with IV = E(KSenc, -1), and PassportReader verifies PK_Map,IC = KA(CA_IC, PK_IC, D_IC) against DG14's ECDH key, surfacing the outcome as PaceCamStatus without ever gating the read. The suite pins the CAM OID dispatch (128/192/256), the ICAO 9303 Part 11 App I.1 handshake values (Brainpool P-256, AES-128: the 7F49 encoding, the mutual-auth tokens and the session keys) and the App I.1 decrypt and verify. App I.1 is the only published CAM chain in existence: the AES-192/256 chains and every negative shape are synthetic, generated by conformance/tools/pace_cam_password_oracle.py, whose self-check recomputes all of App I.1 first. The decrypt/verify ops are dart-only: upstream has no implementation to drive. End-to-end composition is pinned in test/pace_handshake_test.dart (handler -> camData) and test/passport_reader_test.dart (session -> PaceCamStatus); the wire is the same as upstream's, so transcripts/pace_session_cam_aes128.json stays both-runner. Frozen-reference (S3) is needed only for CAM AES-192/256.",
  "runners": [
    "dart",
    "swift"
  ],
